2	// typed build runs. Such a file goes straight to the dynamic build (src/dynpy), which keeps CPython's
3	// behaviour, instead of being built typed and printing something else.
4	//
5	// - Sets: the typed build keeps a set as a list in sorted order, while CPython walks a set in its hash
6	//   table's order (`{-1, 5}` prints `{5, -1}`), and prints it in braces even inside a list or dict. Any
7	//   use where that order or form shows (printing, str(), iterating, list(s), s.pop(), joining) is refused.
8	//   Order-free uses stay typed: `x in s`, len, sorted, min, max, sum, any, all and set algebra.
9	// - print(q, q.popleft()), print(xs, grow(xs)), (q, q.pop()): CPython reads every argument before printing,
10	//   and a tuple or list holds the object itself; the typed build copies each one as it goes, so a later
11	//   argument that changes an earlier one shows the old value.
